# Environment
.env
.env.*
!.env.example

# Bridge webhook targets — carry per-target signing secrets; keep the example only.
bridge/webhooks.json
bridge/webhooks.*.json
!bridge/webhooks.example.json
# Belt-and-braces: a root-level webhooks.json (if the bridge is ever run from the repo root).
/webhooks.json

# The bridge's remembered identity (issue #672) — machine-local runtime state, written on first
# start so consumers can recognise the bridge after its address changes. No secret, but it belongs
# to one installation, so committing it would hand every clone the same identity.
bridge/bridge-id
/bridge-id

# Data artefacts — a real database, dump, backup, or exported snapshot carries live inventory
# data and must never enter this public repo (see the no-secrets rule in CLAUDE.md). These are
# exactly the files a developer drops in the repo root while testing the backup/restore and
# bridge-hydration paths, and the pre-commit hook only scans *added lines* — it cannot flag a
# binary .sqlite or a JSON inventory dump. The only snapshots allowed in git are the synthetic
# fixtures already tracked under src/**/fixtures/ and __fixtures__/ (invented data only).
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
*.db
*.sql
# The bridge's documented input snapshot (real inventory — bridge/README.md) and the app's
# backup archive (gubbins-backup-<stamp>.zip). bridge/.gitignore also covers gubbins-sync.json;
# this catches one produced from the repo root.
gubbins-sync.json
gubbins-backup*.zip

# Keys, certificates, and keystores — never commit a private key or credential store.
*.pem
*.key
*.crt
*.cer
*.pfx
*.p12
*.keystore
*.jks
id_rsa
id_rsa.*
id_dsa
id_ecdsa
id_ed25519

# Local browser-smoke artefact
scripts/.smoke-screenshot.png

# Local checkout of the wiki repo. `docs/todo/wiki_2026-07-11.md` §7 describes running the
# publish sync by hand to see what it would do; that clones the wiki here. It is a whole
# second repo (with its own .git) and must never be committed into this one.
/.wiki/
